Yes. `tblrequestlog` contains high-confidence traces for 5 additional null `tblelement.taxonid` values beyond the original 159:

| Elements | Evidence | Legacy value | Likely current ID |
|---:|---|---|---|
| 3 | Update request names the exact `elementid` | `1616834`, “Pinus longaeva D. K. Bailey” | `77L64` |
| 1 | Update request names the exact `elementid` | `1646865`, “Cedrus libani” | `S24H` |
| 1 | Create request matches parent, code, and creation time within 0.102 seconds | `51976`, “Pseudotsuga” | `73Y7` |

This raises the high-confidence recovery total from 159 to 164 elements. Two of the Pinus mappings also have independent create-time evidence.

There is one additional low-confidence possibility:

- One existing 2015 element matches three 2022 create requests for `Quercus` (`76HN`) by parent and code.
- The six-year timestamp gap suggests these could be rejected duplicate-creation attempts, so I would not apply this automatically.

I also found nine form-log entries containing taxon labels—eight `Pinus longaeva` and one `Plantae`—but they contain no usable element or object identifier and therefore cannot recover additional rows.

