Evidence quality:

- 182 mappings are stated explicitly on individual rows.
- 22 come from clear group statements such as “ACM 262–274 are all Populus.”
- 3 are inherited through explicit “same tree/species as” statements.
- 4 elements are ambiguous and should not be assigned automatically:
  - ACM-14: Abies or Juniperus
  - ACM-162: Quercus or Juniperus
  - ACM-163: Quercus or Buxus
  - ACM-175: Populus or Salix
- 47 database elements have no usable single mapping.

The two documents agree on 117 of the 118 mappings present in both. The sole conflict is ACM-164:

- `ACM.doc`: Populus, identified by Schoch
- `ACMOLD.PRO`: Buxus

I would therefore treat 206 mappings as immediately defensible and send ACM-164 for manual review. None of these ACM elements currently have evidence in `taxonfix`, so these would all be additional recoveries.

This approach should work for similar documents: extract entries, match the object prefix and element code, normalize Latin names against `tlkptaxon`, record provenance and confidence, and emit a reviewable recovery script. No database changes were made.

